    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-106515",
      "knowledge_point_id": "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/999d1d14a31f9619306efe6b1c2ebc6e900a52bf4e2d2edd7230e7dd7c9b25b9.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 2x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(1,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm 2x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\frac{b}{1} & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
